# Runtime state: SQLite, extracted SDK, the serve record and its log
.otter/

# Secrets: never commit an env file, templates included.
#
# There is deliberately no `!*.env.example` re-include here. Git cannot tell a
# filled-in template from a placeholder, and a `.env.example` that someone
# pasted real values into is a secret like any other.
#
#   otter.env    the daemon's credentials, at the repository root
#   .env, *.env  read by nothing; ignored so a stray one cannot be committed
#   .env.*       .env.example, .env.local, .env.production
#   *.env.*      otter.env.example, otter.env.production
#
# otter.env.example is tracked because it was added before these rules existed;
# a new template has to be added with `git add -f`, which is the point.
#
# These are path rules: they cannot tell a filled-in template from a
# placeholder. Keep real values out of any file you intend to commit, and use
# a content-level backstop (gitleaks, trufflehog) for the rest.
.env
.env.*
*.env
*.env.*

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
